    "case_id": "227",
    "case_summary": "A 37-year-old woman with no past medical history presented with continuous chest pain for two hours. She denied dyspnea, syncope, or prior similar episodes. There was no personal or family history of cardiovascular or thromboembolic disease. Vital signs were: pulse 110/min, blood pressure 100/65 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, temperature 37 °C, oxygen saturation 95% on room air (99% on 2 L/min nasal cannula). Cardiac auscultation was normal, and neck veins were not distended. Initial ECG showed sinus rhythm with 2 mm of ST-segment elevation in leads V2 and V3. She was given aspirin, clopidogrel, and intravenous heparin and taken to the catheterization laboratory. Twenty minutes later, a second ECG showed a new right bundle branch block and resolution of the precordial ST-segment elevations. Forty-five minutes after presentation, coronary angiography revealed normal coronary arteries without stenosis. Transthoracic echocardiography demonstrated right ventricular dilatation and an estimated pulmonary arterial pressure of 60 mmHg.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. pulmonary embolism\nB. elevation of the ST segment is wide including conditions with secondary of the myocardium … pre existing ST elevation\nC. Acute myocardial infarction\nD. Preexisting ST elevation without acute ischemia\nE. Myocarditis or pericarditis\nF. Electrolyte imbalance\nG. Rate\nH. Aortic dissection\n",
